            <img src="/images/posting/celebrity-posting-sites.png" width="1200" height="675" alt={title} loading="eager" decoding="async" />
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="content-body">
        <div class="container article-shell">
          <div class="prose pillar-body">
            <p class="prose-lede">Entertainment publishing splits into four tiers, and where you can land a byline depends on whether you have reporting, criticism or just an opinion.</p>

            <p>The celebrity news brands do not take guest posts. The pop-culture and film outlets take contributed criticism. The music publications want writers with a track record. The lifestyle titles have a celebrity desk that takes expert commentary. The list of 100 is below, grouped by tier.</p>

            <h2 id="tier">Pick your tier first</h2>
            <figure>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>Tier</th><th>Examples</th><th>How you get in</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td>Celebrity news brands</td><td>People, TMZ, E! Online, Us Weekly, Page Six, the tabloids</td><td>Not guest posts. A verifiable exclusive, or expert commentary from a stylist, publicist or entertainment lawyer</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Pop-culture &amp; film</td><td>Vulture, IndieWire, Collider, ScreenRant, Slashfilm, The Playlist</td><td>Contributed criticism and analysis from writers with published clips</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Music publications</td><td>Rolling Stone, Billboard, Pitchfork, NME, Stereogum, Consequence, Loudwire</td><td>Reviews, features and data pieces from writers with a music-journalism track record</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Lifestyle titles with a celeb desk</td><td>Cosmopolitan, Glamour, Marie Claire, InStyle, Refinery29, PopSugar</td><td>Expert commentary tied to a celebrity moment; styling, wellness, culture angles</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <h2 id="metrics">What the metrics mean</h2>
            <figure>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>Metric</th><th>What it is</th><th>How much it matters</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td>Domain Rating (DR)</td><td>Ahrefs' 0 to 100 score for a whole site's backlink profile</td><td>A useful filter, not a verdict</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Domain Authority (DA)</td><td>Moz's equivalent 0 to 100 score</td><td>Same idea as DR</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Page Authority (PA)</td><td>Moz's score for one specific page</td><td>Matters once you know which page your post will live on</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>URL Rating (UR)</td><td>Ahrefs' score for one specific URL</td><td>Same purpose as PA</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Monthly Traffic</td><td>Estimated organic visits per month</td><td>The most honest signal that a link will be seen</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Spam Score</td><td>Moz's 0 to 4 estimate of how many spam signals a site shows</td><td>The celebrity-gossip end of this niche runs hot; treat 3 as a warning</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <div class="callout"><p><strong>Here is the deal:</strong> the top of this list is genuine national media. The bottom is thin gossip content that Google has hit in updates and that trades links freely. A placement on Vulture or The Wrap is worth more than fifty on gossip aggregators, and the fifty carry risk.</p></div>

            <h2 id="list">The 100 celebrity sites</h2>
            <p>Domain Rating, Domain Authority, Page Authority, URL Rating, estimated monthly traffic and Spam Score. Every domain was checked live in September 2026; closed and merged sites were swapped for live equivalents. Metrics are third-party estimates and shift over time.</p>
            <figure>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>#</th><th>Website</th><th>DR</th><th>DA</th><th>PA</th><th>UR</th><th>Monthly traffic</th><th>Spam</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  {SITES.map((s) => (
                    <tr>
                      <td>{s.n}</td>
                      <td>{s.site}</td>
                      <td>{s.dr}</td>
                      <td>{s.da}</td>
                      <td>{s.pa}</td>
                      <td>{s.ur}</td>
                      <td>{s.traffic}</td>
                      <td>{s.spam}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))}
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <h2 id="pitch">The pitch that works for entertainment editors</h2>
            <ol>
              <li><strong>Lead with the reporting or the angle.</strong> "I have three sources on the reshoots" or "why this casting says something about the whole franchise" earns the read.</li>
              <li><strong>Show your clips.</strong> Published criticism or reporting. Entertainment editors will not take an unknown for a byline.</li>
              <li><strong>Pitch the section.</strong> "For your TV criticism" or "for the music features desk" shows you read the publication.</li>
              <li><strong>Match the format.</strong> News wants a tight, sourced story. Culture sites want an argued essay. Music sites want a review or a data feature.</li>
              <li><strong>Bring the assets.</strong> Box-office or streaming data, chart analysis, an interview transcript.</li>
              <li><strong>Disclose any client, studio or PR relationship</strong> up front.</li>
            </ol>
            <div class="callout"><p><strong>The test:</strong> would an editor run this if you were not promoting anyone? If yes, pitch it. If it exists to push a client, it goes in the bin.</p></div>

            <h2 id="mistakes">What not to do</h2>
            <ul class="check-list">
              <li>Pitch People or TMZ a "guest post." That format does not exist there.</li>
              <li>Buy placements on gossip aggregators that quote a per-post price.</li>
              <li>Send a press release dressed as a "feature."</li>
              <li>Put exact-match anchors ("celebrity news") on links back to your client's site.</li>
              <li>Submit AI-generated hot takes with no reporting and no named writer.</li>
            </ul>
            <div class="callout"><p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> a short list of placements on real entertainment media beats a long list of gossip-site links every time, and it is the only approach that does not put the domain at risk. Use this list to find the ones that count.</p></div>
          </div>

          <aside class="article-toc">
            <h4>On this page</h4>
            <ul>
              {toc.map((item) => (
                <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.text}</a></li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </aside>
        </div>
      </section>

      <div id="faq">
